From 94b6f6610ae35d2aa7ca8e243742cf46ddafe2ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Denguinho <k@local>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 18:47:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA: env-gated small-batch MMQ grid multiplier and y-tile
 double buffer

Measured on the RTX 3090 with Qwen3.8-27B, a speculative verify batch of 2-8
tokens lands on the J=8 MMQ tile and pays a fixed ~31 us per call that the
arithmetic does not explain. ncu attributes it to memory-level parallelism:
the stream-k grid is nsm blocks, so such a launch runs one block per SM, 8 of
48 possible warps, 6.19 warp cycles per issued instruction and 56% of DRAM
peak, while registers and shared memory each permit more blocks. The grid is
what binds, not either resource.

GGML_CUDA_MMQ_SMALLN, restricted to NVIDIA Ampere and to J <= 8, each level a
superset of the one below:

  1  raise the stream-k grid for J <= 8 from nsm to nsm*min(8, smpbo/nbytes),
     i.e. as many blocks per SM as shared memory actually allows.
  2  additionally double-buffer the y tile so both halves are loaded before a
     single barrier and both vec_dot calls run between two barriers: two
     __syncthreads per k-iteration instead of four. Three of the stock four
     exist only because tile_y is reused across the two vec_dot calls, which
     is safe to change because vec_dot addresses y base-relative and k00
     indexes only the x tile.
  3  additionally walk the grid back where the multiplier costs more than it
     buys. Splitting an output tile across more blocks eventually leaves a
     block with too little of the k dimension to amortise its prologue and
     the stream-k fixup reduction. Measured at qwen's shapes, m=1024 gets
     0.98 MMQ_ITER_K iterations of work per block and regresses 19%, while
     the next lowest shape gets 5.85 and gains; the level keeps at least 4 and
     never drops below the stock grid. The threshold is in iterations, not k
     blocks: a k block is qk elements, so an iteration covers MMQ_ITER_K/qk of
     them, 8 for Q4_0/Q8_0 but 1 for the K quants. A fixed k-block threshold
     is 8x too aggressive on Q4_K/Q6_K and was measured costing attn_gate 7%.

Level 2 costs one more padded y tile of shared memory, 1536 B at J=8. That
does not change the block count level 1 computes (21024 -> 22560 B still
divides smpbo four times), so the two compose; a register-pipelined x tile,
measured separately, does not, because its 158 registers cap the launch at one
block per SM. Level 3 changes only the grid, so it adds no instantiation and
no registers: ptxas reports 89/139 at levels 0 and 1 and 91/139 at levels 2
and 3 for mul_mat_q<Q4_0, J=8>.

The small-J entries of the Ampere config table also move from 256 threads /
I=128 to 128 / I=64 unconditionally, halving the tile so more blocks fit. I is
pinned to nthreads/2 by the warp-to-row mapping, so the two must move together.
This means level 0 in this build is not the upstream kernel; the env gates only
the grid multiplier, the y buffer and the shape gate. One shape regresses from
that table change alone and is not recoverable by any grid predicate: ffn_down
(m=5120, k=17408) costs 1.086x stock at every level, including with the grid
multiplier off. Fixing it needs I/nthreads to become runtime-selectable.
